{{Main|Constrained Capitalism|Constrained Capitalism (Okaiken)|Constrained Capitalism (Yanbaru)}}
{{Nihongo|'''Constrained Capitalism'''|制限資本主義|Seigen Shihonshugi}} is the dominant socioeconomic system of [[Nakamizu]]. The system combines a market-oriented capitalist economy with an extensive social welfare framework, strategic state intervention, and regulatory oversight intended to reduce social inequality and maintain long-term economic stability. It emerged during the post-[[Great Adonian War]] reconstruction period of the 1950s and became the guiding economic doctrine of the country throughout the late 20th century.

The model is commonly characterized as a hybrid between [[w:Neoliberal|neoliberal]] economic policies and the [[w:Nordic model|welfare state]]. Supporters of the system argue that it enabled rapid industrialization, rising living standards, and social cohesion, while critics contend that the model relies heavily on bureaucratic management, high taxation, and close relationships between the state and major corporate groups.

By the early 21st century, Constrained Capitalism was widely credited with transforming Nakamizu from an impoverished country into one of [[Adonia]]'s largest economies and a leading technological and industrial power. Alongside similar systems in neighboring [[Okaiken]] and [[Yanbaru]], the Nakamian variant forms one of three distinctive models collectively known as the [[Fosian Tigers]].

==Characteristics==
Constrained Capitalism is defined by the coexistence of competitive private enterprise and a broad social safety net. While markets remain largely liberalized and export-oriented, the state maintains an active role in regulating labor relations, infrastructure development, education, healthcare, and strategic industries.

Key features of the system include:

* Universal healthcare and near-universal social insurance coverage
* Extensive public investment in transportation, housing, and education
* Strong labor protections combined with flexible corporate hiring practices
* Industry-wide labor unions, contrasting with Okaiken's enterprise-based model
* Export-oriented industrial policy coordinated through public-private councils
* Progressive taxation and relatively high public spending (~48% of GDP)
* Public-private coordination in strategic sectors
* Limited but targeted state ownership in key industries
* Emphasis on consensus-based labor relations between government, business federations, and trade unions

The system also places strong cultural emphasis on social harmony, economic pragmatism, and national developmental goals. Economists have described the model as balancing market competitiveness with social stabilization mechanisms intended to mitigate the effects of economic inequality and cyclical downturns.

===Comparison with Okaiken===
{{Main|Constrained Capitalism (Okaiken)|Okaiken-Nakamizu relations}}

Nakamizu's Constrained Capitalism differs significantly from the model adopted by northern neighbor [[Okaiken]], despite shared Sanese cultural foundations and similar post-war reconstruction challenges. These differences reflect divergent political traditions dating to the [[Schism of the Dual Suns]] of 1336 and the separate modernization paths taken during the 19th century.

